  "prompt": "Evaluate $$\\int_{-2}^{2}\\frac{4+\\sin(2x)}{4+x^2}\\,dx.$$",
  "image": null,
  "choices": null,
  "answer": "pi",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "answerDisplay": "$\\pi$",
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Odd part vanishes",
    "body": "Split into $\\int_{-2}^2\\frac{4}{4+x^2}dx+\\int_{-2}^2\\frac{\\sin2x}{4+x^2}dx$. The second integrand is odd, so its integral over $[-2,2]$ is $0$. For the first, $\\frac4{4+x^2}=\\frac1{1+(x/2)^2}$ has antiderivative $2\\arctan\\frac x2$, giving $2\\left(\\frac\\pi4+\\frac\\pi4\\right)=\\pi$.\n\n**Answer: $\\pi$**"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
