  "type": "numeric",
  "prompt": "Evaluate $$\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2-\\cos\\theta}\\,d\\theta.$$",
  "image": null,
  "choices": null,
  "answer": "3*pi/4",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "answerDisplay": "$\\frac{3\\pi}{4}$",
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Weierstrass substitution",
    "body": "Let $t=\\tan\\frac\\theta2$, so $\\cos\\theta=\\frac{1-t^2}{1+t^2}$, $d\\theta=\\frac{2dt}{1+t^2}$ and $t$ runs over $[0,1]$. The integral becomes $\\int_0^1\\frac{2\\,dt}{(\\sqrt2+1)t^2+(\\sqrt2-1)}=\\int_0^1\\frac{2(\\sqrt2-1)\\,dt}{t^2+(\\sqrt2-1)^2}$ (multiplying numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt2-1$) $=2\\arctan\\frac t{\\sqrt2-1}\\Big|_0^1=2\\arctan(\\sqrt2+1)$.\n\nSince $\\tan\\frac{3\\pi}8=1+\\sqrt2$, this equals $2\\cdot\\frac{3\\pi}8=\\frac{3\\pi}4$.\n\n**Answer: $\\frac{3\\pi}4$** (the official answer is written $2\\arctan(\\sqrt2+1)$, the same number)"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
   "calculus"
  ],
  "difficulty": 3,
